{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Hear, O Israel — you are crossing, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>Hear, O Israel</b> means: give your minds to this statement that I say to you, ״you are crossing״ — what is my intention in saying to you ״you are crossing,״ and grasp what my intention in this statement is. And he upon him be peace intended to alert them to what had befallen him, namely that he is not crossing, and therefore he said <b>you</b> — to say, and not I. And the reason is as they said, of blessed memory (Deuteronomy Rabbah 3:11), and this is their language: to open for them an opening through which they might seek mercy upon him — end quote. And he suspected that perhaps they would not understand his words, so he prefaced by saying ״Hear,״ meaning, understand. And the verse's saying <b>this day</b> intends to say that the separation between them and him — that they are crossing and he is not crossing — is specifically this day, but at another time he too will cross, as they said, of blessed memory (Zohar 2:120a) on the verse ״כִּימֵי צֵאתְךָ״ etc., for in the future Moses will bring Israel into the land of Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The abbreviation 'וגו׳' (etc.) is retained as a cut-off marker, following the convention of representing truncated quotations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:08.151788249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}